course: everyday
lesson: 21
title: Reading a dog's body language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dogs cannot talk, but they tell us how they feel all the time with their tail, ears, mouth, eyes and whole body.
explain: A relaxed, friendly dog has a loose, wiggly body, a soft open mouth and a tail that wags in wide, easy sweeps.
explain: A frightened or angry dog goes stiff and still: its tail may be tucked low or held high and rigid, its ears flat back or stiffly forward, and it may show the whites of its eyes, curl its lip or growl.
explain: A wagging tail does not always mean a happy dog; a stiff, high tail that flicks fast can mean the dog is tense and ready to act.
explain: A growl is a polite warning that means «stop, I don't like this»: the safe thing is to stop what you are doing and move slowly away.
explain: For example, a dog lying under a table with its ears back, licking its lips and showing the whites of its eyes is telling you: «Leave me alone.»
explain: A family dog in Ohio had been gentle for years; when a toddler grabbed its tail it bit her face and she needed stitches.
explain: A boy in Texas kept patting a dog that had gone stiff, licked its lips and showed the whites of its eyes; it snapped and bit his hand.
fact: Dogs show their feelings with their whole body, not only with their tail.
fact: A loose, wiggly body and a soft, open mouth usually mean a relaxed, friendly dog.
fact: A stiff body that suddenly freezes is a warning sign that a bite may come next.
fact: A tail tucked between the back legs usually means the dog is frightened.
fact: A tail held high and stiff, even if it wags, can mean the dog is tense or excited, not friendly.
fact: Ears pinned flat back often mean fear; ears pushed stiffly forward can mean the dog is on alert.
fact: Showing the whites of the eyes, lip licking and yawning when not tired are signs of a worried dog.
fact: A growl, a curled lip or bared teeth are warnings that mean «back off».
fact: Hair standing up along a dog's back means it is upset, scared or very excited.
fact: A play bow, with the front legs down and the bottom up, is a dog's invitation to play.
fact: Most dog bites happen with dogs the child already knows, often their own family dog; even a friendly dog may bite when it is scared, hurt or annoyed.
fact: Dog bites send thousands of children to hospital every year, and children are often bitten on the face.
fact: Most dogs that bite children are dogs they know, at home or at a friend's house.
exercise: Which body shows a relaxed, friendly dog? | A loose, wiggly body with a soft open mouth | A stiff body that suddenly goes still | A low body with the tail tucked right under | A tense body with the hair raised along the back
exercise: What does a tail tucked between the back legs usually mean? | The dog is frightened | The dog wants to play with you | The dog is very hungry | The dog is very happy indeed
exercise: Does a wagging tail always mean a dog is happy? | No, a stiff, high wag can mean it is tense | Yes, a wagging tail always means it is happy | Yes, but only if the dog is a small breed | No, a wagging tail always means it is angry
exercise: What does a dog's growl mean? | It is a warning to stop and back away | It means the dog wants to be stroked | It is the sound a dog makes when it laughs | It means the dog is about to fall asleep
exercise: A dog suddenly freezes and goes stiff while you pat it. What should you do? | Stop patting and slowly move away | Pat it harder and faster to calm it down | Hug it so it feels safe | Stare at it until it relaxes
exercise: What is a play bow? | Front legs down and bottom up, an invitation to play | A low bow a dog always makes just before it bites someone | A way of showing it is tired | A sign that the dog is in pain
exercise: What do ears pinned flat back often mean? | The dog is afraid | The dog is curious and alert | The dog is calm and sleepy | The dog wants food
exercise: A dog is showing the whites of its eyes and licking its lips. How does it probably feel? | Worried and uncomfortable | Happy and ready to play a game | Sleepy and relaxed | Hungry and excited
exercise: Hair is standing up along a dog's back. What does this tell you? | The dog is upset, scared or very excited | The dog is cold and wants a warm coat to wear | The dog has just had a bath | The dog is calm and friendly
exercise: A dog curls its lip and shows its teeth at you. What is it saying? | Back off, I don't like this | Hello, I am smiling at you | I am hungry, give me food | Please throw my ball
exercise: Which dog is a child most likely to be bitten by? | A dog the child already knows | A dog the child has never met before | A dog seen once in a busy street | A dog met only once in a big park
exercise: A dog yawns again and again while a crowd of children pat it. What is it most likely saying? | I feel stressed, give me space | I am bored, keep patting me | I am very sleepy and happy | I want everyone to stay close to me
exercise: Why should you never tell a dog off for growling? | The growl is its warning, and without it a dog may bite with no warning | Growling dogs always enjoy being told off, so it does them no good at all | Telling it off makes it growl more happily | A growl is just a cough and means nothing
test: Max, a dog in Texas, is lying very still with his ears back and his tail tucked under. What is Max saying? | I am scared, leave me alone | Come and play with me now | I am happy to see you | Please give me a big hug
test: Which is true? | A dog can wag its tail and still be tense | A wagging tail always means a friendly dog | Only a growl can ever show a dog's feelings | A dog's ears never show you how it is feeling
test: A puppy drops its front legs to the floor, sticks its bottom up and wiggles. What does it want? | To play with you | To jump up and bite you | To be left all alone | To go off to sleep
test: A boy in Leeds hears his grandma's dog growl when he pulls its toy. What is the best thing to do? | Let go of the toy and move away calmly | Pull the toy harder so the dog knows you win | Shout at the dog to be quiet | Hug the dog to say sorry
test: Which of these is a warning sign that a dog may bite? | Its body suddenly stiffens and goes still | Its body is loose and wiggly all over, like jelly | It rolls over to play | It wags its tail with its whole bottom
test: Two dogs meet in a park in Sydney. One has its tail held high and stiff, its body tense and its hair raised. How does it feel? | Tense and ready for trouble | Calm and sleepy | Shy and wanting to hide | Bored and uninterested
test: What can a dog do to show it is worried, apart from growling? | Lick its lips, yawn or show the whites of its eyes | Wag its whole body and bounce about | Lie on its back with a soft, relaxed and very happy face | Run up with a loose, open mouth
test: Why is it useful to watch a dog's whole body, not just its tail? | The tail alone can fool you about its mood | Only the tail ever shows a dog's mood | Dogs keep their tails still when they are angry | The body never changes when a dog feels scared
test: A girl in Ohio thinks: «Our dog knows me, so it will never bite me.» What is wrong with her idea? | Most bites come from dogs children already know | Dogs never bite anyone they have met before, only strangers | Only very large dogs ever bite people | Family dogs cannot feel scared or annoyed
test: dog's wagging, so he's friendly yeah? | Not always, look at the whole body | Yes, wagging always means it's friendly | Yes, as long as the tail is held high | Yes, unless it's barking its head off
test: dog went all stiff n froze when i got near | Back away, a bite may come next | Pat it gently to calm it down | Hug it, it is scared | Run off quickly
test: dog did that bum in the air front legs down thing | It wants to play | It is about to bite | It is very tired out | It is hurt somewhere
test: our own dog won't bite me tho | Most bites come from dogs kids know | Family dogs never bite | Only big dogs bite | Only strange dogs bite, never your own
test: dog's gone stiff and is showing the whites of its eyes, still wanna pat him | Stop and step back slowly, he may bite | Pat him faster to calm him | Hug him so he feels safe | Look straight in his eyes
test: grandma's dog bit my cousin, but he's always been nice?? | Even friendly dogs bite when scared or hurt | He must be a bad dog now | He was only trying to play | Dogs only bite people they do not know
test: dog growled when i went near his food, whats that mean | Leave him alone, he is warning you to back off | He wants you to share it | He is saying hello | He is hungry and wants you to give him more food
test: did u hear the Smiths' dog bit a kid even tho it was wagging its tail the whole time, mental | Real lesson: a stiff, high wagging tail can mean tension, so read the whole body | Real lesson: dogs that wag never bite, so the story is false | It proves every dog with a tail bites | It means only the dog's face shows its feelings
test: my mate says if a dog yawns it's just bored and u can cuddle it | Not quite; a yawn when not tired can mean the dog is worried, so give it space | He is right, a yawning dog wants a cuddle | He is right, yawning means it is about to play and wants you to join in the game | He is right, yawning dogs are always sleepy
test: they're saying the new kid's dog is vicious and he trains it to attack, pass it round | Do not pass it round; it is likely a rumour and could hurt him and his family | Pass it round, so nobody goes near his house | Pass it round, because dogs are always dangerous | Pass it round, and say the dog has bitten before
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a dog's body becoming stiff and suddenly motionless? | It constitutes a warning that a bite may follow | It signifies an invitation to play | It indicates that the dog wishes to be stroked at once | It signifies that the dog is about to sleep
exam: Identify the meaning ordinarily conveyed by a tail held tucked between the hind legs. | That the dog is frightened | That the dog is relaxed and friendly | That the dog is hungry | That the dog is about to fetch a ball
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the play bow. | The front legs are lowered and the hindquarters raised, constituting an invitation to play | The dog lies flat upon its back as a sign of anger | The dog raises its tail stiffly as a warning | The dog shows the whites of its eyes as a greeting
exam: It is given that a dog's hair stands erect along its back. Determine the most probable meaning thereof. | That the dog is upset, frightened or highly excited | That the dog is entirely calm and contented | That the dog is cold and requires a blanket at once | That the dog wishes to be fed immediately
